INT. FISH MARKET OFFICE - 5 A.M.

Fluorescent tube. Ice buckets. Stacked styrofoam crates reeking of brine. A cracked window shows the dark harbor.

MR. KOVACS, 60s, grey suit, sits behind a metal desk. STELLA, 30s, sleek in a trench coat, leans against a filing cabinet. NUNZIO, 40s, apron smeared with blood, wipes a cleaver on a rag. SPUDS, 20s, jittery, bounces his knee. MABEL, 50s, bookkeeper, spectacles on a chain, sorts receipts. DEACON, 70s, white-haired, in a church coat, sits quietly by the door with a fishing basket on his lap.

KOVACS
Where's the money, Spuds?

SPUDS
I don't have it, Mr. K.

NUNZIO
He says that every week.

STELLA
Because every week it's true.

SPUDS
Hey! I'm right here.

MABEL
Spuds, dear, you're nineteen thousand short.

SPUDS
Mabel, you know I love you...

MABEL
Don't "Mabel" me.

KOVACS
Quiet, all of you.

The room freezes. Kovacs turns to Deacon, who has not moved.

KOVACS (CONT'D)
Deacon. Open the basket.

DEACON
It's only fish, Mr. K.

KOVACS
Open it.

Deacon lifts the lid. Inside, wrapped in newsprint, rows of banded bills. Spuds gasps. Stella steps back. Nunzio lowers the cleaver. Mabel drops her pen.

DEACON
I was keeping it safe.

KOVACS
For whom?

DEACON
For Spuds. The boy's got a baby coming.

SPUDS
You what?

DEACON
Don't thank me yet.
